Imports:
  - Types:
      - arrayToMap
      - Emitter
    From: packages/common/src
  - Types:
      - GlobalPoint
      - LocalPoint
    From: packages/math/src
  - Types:
      - generateNKeysBetween
    From: packages/fractional-indexing/src

Usages:
  host_app_state: |
    Several functions here (binding, frame membership, resizing/snapping, the Store
    constructor) accept a parameter shaped like the host application's runtime state (current
    tool, selection, viewport, style defaults) or the host App instance itself. This cell
    treats that parameter opaquely — by shape, not by importing a concrete type from the
    application cell — so it stays free of a dependency edge back onto the cell that composes
    it (packages/excalidraw, which itself depends on this cell for the element/scene model).
  ordering_invariant: |
    Every element in a scene must carry a valid, correctly-ordered index (a fractional-index
    string from packages/fractional-indexing/src, wrapped in the FractionalIndex brand via
    packages/math/src-style branding). Use `syncMovedIndices`/syncInvalidIndices any time
    elements are inserted, reordered, or restored from an external source, rather than writing
    to index directly.

Annotations: |
  This cell is Excalidraw's element & scene data model: the `ExcalidrawElement` type hierarchy,
  the `Scene` class that indexes all elements of a drawing, mutation/duplication/creation of
  elements, arrow-to-shape binding, grouping, resizing, multi-point line editing, and the
  fractional-index-based ordering of elements. It is the single most widely depended-upon cell
  after packages/common/src.

  Use `arrayToMap` (from Imports) for id-keyed lookups over element lists, matching this cell's
  own convention. Use `host_app_state` when a function needs to read host application state.
  Use `ordering_invariant` when writing any logic that inserts, moves, or restores elements.

---

"ExcalidrawElement()":
  location: types.ts
  annotations: |
    The base shape shared by every canvas element, discriminated by a type field into ~14
    concrete variants (rectangle, diamond, ellipse, text, line, arrow — including an "elbowed"
    arrow sub-variant with orthogonal routing, freedraw, image, frame, magicframe, iframe,
    embeddable, selection). Every field below is common to all variants; each variant adds a
    handful of type-specific fields (e.g. text elements add fontSize/text, arrows add
    points/startBinding/endBinding).
  properties:
    "id -> string": |
      Stable unique identifier.
    "type -> string": |
      The discriminant selecting which concrete element variant this is.
    "x -> number": |
      "y -> number": |
      Top-left position in scene (global) coordinates.
    "width -> number": |
      "height -> number": |
      Bounding box size.
    "angle -> Radians": |
      Rotation around the element's own center.
    "index -> string | null": |
      This element's fractional-index ordering key; see `ordering_invariant`.
    "version -> number": |
      "versionNonce -> number": |
      Monotonic change counters bumped on every mutation; used for conflict resolution when
      merging concurrent edits and for cheap "did this change" comparisons.
    "isDeleted -> boolean": |
      Soft-delete flag; deleted elements are retained (for undo/collaboration) rather than
      removed from the elements array.
    "groupIds -> Array<string>": |
      Ids of the groups this element belongs to, innermost group first.
    "boundElements -> Array<{ id: string, type: string }> | null": |
      Other elements bound to this one (e.g. bound text, or arrows bound to this shape).

"Scene(elements: Array<ExcalidrawElement> | null)":
  location: Scene.ts
  annotations: |
    The single in-memory index of every element of a drawing (including soft-deleted ones),
    with derived views (non-deleted elements, frame-like elements, an id-to-element map) and a
    change-notification channel so UI re-renders when the scene changes.

    Algorithm:
    1. On construction or replaceAllElements, rebuild the id-to-element map (via `arrayToMap`
       from Imports) and all derived views from the given element list
    2. Any call to this cell's `mutateElement` routine through Scene.mutateElement also bumps
       sceneNonce and, if the element's version actually changed, calls triggerUpdate
    3. triggerUpdate fires every subscriber registered via onUpdate (an `Emitter`, from
       Imports, under the hood) with the new scene nonce
  methods:
    "getNonDeletedElements() -> elements:Array<ExcalidrawElement>": |
      All elements with isDeleted: false.
    "getElementsIncludingDeleted() -> elements:Array<ExcalidrawElement>": |
      Every element, including soft-deleted ones.
    "getElement(id: string) -> element:ExcalidrawElement | null": |
      Look up a single element by id.
    "getSelectedElements(opts: { selectedElementIds: Record<string, boolean>, includeBoundTextElement: boolean, includeElementsInFrames: boolean }) -> elements:Array<ExcalidrawElement>": |
      Resolve the currently-selected id set to actual elements, optionally expanding bound text
      and frame children.
    "replaceAllElements(nextElements: Array<ExcalidrawElement>)": |
      Replace the entire scene contents and trigger an update.
    "insertElementsAtIndex(elements: Array<ExcalidrawElement>, index: number | null)": |
      Low-level insertion at a specific array position; most callers should go through element
      creation helpers instead, which also assign a correct fractional index (see
      `ordering_invariant`).
    "onUpdate(cb: Function) -> remove:Function": |
      Subscribe to scene-change notifications; returns an unsubscribe callback.
    "triggerUpdate()": |
      Bump the scene nonce and notify all subscribers.
    "mutateElement(element: ExcalidrawElement, updates: Record<string, any>, options: { informMutation: boolean, isDragging: boolean }) -> element:ExcalidrawElement": |
      Apply `updates` to `element` in place and, when `informMutation` is set and the version
      changed, call triggerUpdate.

"mutateElement(element: ExcalidrawElement, elementsMap: Map<string, ExcalidrawElement>, updates: Record<string, any>) -> element:ExcalidrawElement":
  location: mutateElement.ts
  annotations: |
    In-place element mutation: assigns only the fields present in `updates`, bumps
    version/versionNonce, and invalidates any cached render shape for the element.

    `updates`: partial field set to apply, excluding id/updated
    `element`: the same object reference, mutated

    Constraints:
    - Does not itself trigger a UI update — use Scene.mutateElement when a re-render is needed
    - Prefer newElementWith (same file) instead when an immutable, new-object-reference result
      is required rather than in-place mutation

"Store(app: any)":
  location: store.ts
  annotations: |
    Captures observed element/app-state changes and emits them as increments consumed by
    packages/excalidraw's undo/redo History. Distinguishes durable increments (worth an undo
    step) from ephemeral ones (transient UI state not worth undoing).

    `app`: the host application instance; see `host_app_state`
  methods:
    "scheduleCapture()": |
      Mark that the next commit should capture a durable increment immediately.
    "scheduleAction(action: string)": |
      Schedule one of the three capture strategies (immediate / never / eventually-coalesced)
      for the next commit.
    "commit(elements: Map<string, ExcalidrawElement> | undefined, appState: Record<string, any> | undefined)": |
      Diff the given elements/appState against the last snapshot per the scheduled action, and
      emit the resulting increment(s).
    "clear()": |
      Reset to an empty snapshot with no pending scheduled action.

"bindOrUnbindBindingElements(selectedArrows: Array<ExcalidrawElement>, scene: Scene, appState: any)":
  location: binding.ts
  annotations: |
    For each selected arrow, (re)compute whether it should bind to a shape under its endpoint or
    become unbound, based on current pointer/element geometry.

    `appState`: see `host_app_state`

"updateBoundElements(changedElement: ExcalidrawElement, scene: Scene)":
  location: binding.ts
  annotations: |
    When a bindable shape changes (moves/resizes), recompute the position of every arrow and
    bound text element attached to it.

"fixBindingsAfterDeletion(sceneElements: Array<ExcalidrawElement>, deletedElements: Array<ExcalidrawElement>)":
  location: binding.ts
  annotations: |
    Remove now-dangling binding references (arrow endpoints, bound text) left behind after
    `deletedElements` were removed from `sceneElements`.

"selectGroup(groupId: string, appState: any, elements: Array<ExcalidrawElement>) -> selection:Record<string, any>":
  location: groups.ts
  annotations: |
    Compute the selection state (selected element ids, selected group ids, editing group)
    resulting from clicking on an element that belongs to `groupId`.

    `appState`: see `host_app_state`

"addToGroup(prevGroupIds: Array<string>, newGroupId: string, editingGroupId: string | null) -> groupIds:Array<string>":
  location: groups.ts
  annotations: |
    Append `newGroupId` to an element's existing group-id chain at the correct nesting position.

"duplicateElements(opts: { elements: Array<ExcalidrawElement>, randomizeSeed: boolean }) -> result:{ duplicatedElements: Array<ExcalidrawElement>, elementsWithDuplicates: Array<ExcalidrawElement> }":
  location: duplicate.ts
  annotations: |
    Duplicate a set of elements, either as a full copy placed elsewhere ("everything" mode) or
    in-place at their original positions with new ids ("in-place" mode used by drag-duplicate).
    Preserves and remaps group membership and frame membership across the duplicated set.

"newElement(opts: { type: string, x: number, y: number }) -> element:ExcalidrawElement":
  location: newElement.ts
  annotations: |
    Construct a new, non-deleted generic element (rectangle/diamond/ellipse) with sensible
    defaults for every field this cell's `ExcalidrawElement` type requires. Sibling functions in
    the same file (newTextElement, newArrowElement, newFrameElement, newFreeDrawElement,
    newImageElement) follow the same pattern for their respective variants.

"transformElements(originalElements: Record<string, ExcalidrawElement>, transformHandleType: string, selectedElements: Array<ExcalidrawElement>, scene: Scene, shouldMaintainAspectRatio: boolean, pointerX: number, pointerY: number) -> handled:boolean":
  location: resizeElements.ts
  annotations: |
    Top-level entry point for interactive resize/rotate: given the drag handle being used and
    the current pointer position, dispatches to single- or multi-element resize/rotate logic and
    applies the result via this cell's `mutateElement`.

"syncMovedIndices(elements: Array<ExcalidrawElement>, movedElements: Map<string, ExcalidrawElement>) -> elements:Array<ExcalidrawElement>":
  location: fractionalIndex.ts
  annotations: |
    After `movedElements` change position in the array, assign each one a new fractional index
    that preserves the array order, using `generateNKeysBetween` (from Imports) for the gaps
    between unmoved neighbors; falls back to a full re-index (syncInvalidIndices, same file) if
    the moved set can't be resolved cleanly.

    See `ordering_invariant`.

"LinearElementEditor(element: ExcalidrawElement)":
  location: linearElementEditor.ts
  annotations: |
    Drives interactive multi-point editing of line/arrow elements: dragging an existing point,
    adding or deleting a point, box-selecting a subset of points, and positioning bound text
    along the line. Most of its surface is exposed as functions that take the element plus a
    `Scene` rather than as instance state.
  methods:
    "movePoints(element: ExcalidrawElement, scene: Scene, pointUpdates: Map<number, { point: GlobalPoint | LocalPoint }>)": |
      Move a subset of the element's points to new coordinates, re-binding endpoints as needed.
    "addPoints(element: ExcalidrawElement, scene: Scene, addedPoints: Array<LocalPoint>)": |
      Append new points to the line/arrow.
    "deletePoints(element: ExcalidrawElement, scene: Scene, pointIndices: Array<number>)": |
      Remove points by index, keeping at least the two endpoints.
    "getPointIndexUnderCursor(element: ExcalidrawElement, elementsMap: Map<string, ExcalidrawElement>, zoom: number, x: number, y: number) -> index:number": |
      Hit-test which point (if any) the cursor is currently over, for drag/hover interactions.

"getFrameChildren(elements: Array<ExcalidrawElement>, frameId: string) -> children:Array<ExcalidrawElement>":
  location: frame.ts
  annotations: |
    All non-deleted elements currently assigned to frame frameId.

"addElementsToFrame(elements: Array<ExcalidrawElement>, elementsToAdd: Array<ExcalidrawElement>, frame: ExcalidrawElement) -> elements:Array<ExcalidrawElement>":
  location: frame.ts
  annotations: |
    Assign `elementsToAdd` to `frame`'s membership (setting their frameId), applying frame
    clipping rules.

